msgid "Examples of proper LED names:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:88
msgid "\"red:disk\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:89
msgid "\"white:flash\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:90
msgid "\"red:indicator\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:91
msgid "\"phy1:green:wlan\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:92
msgid "\"phy3::wlan\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:93
msgid "\":kbd_backlight\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:94
msgid "\"input5::kbd_backlight\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:95
msgid "\"input3::numlock\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:96
msgid "\"input3::scrolllock\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:97
msgid "\"input3::capslock\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:98
msgid "\"mmc1::status\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:99
msgid "\"white:status\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:101
msgid ""
"get_led_device_info.sh script can be used for verifying if the LED name "
"meets the requirements pointed out here. It performs validation of the LED "
"class devicename sections and gives hints on expected value for a section in "
"case the validation fails for it. So far the script supports validation of "
"associations between LEDs and following types of devices:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:107
msgid "input devices"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:108
msgid "ieee80211 compliant USB devices"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:110
msgid "The script is open to extensions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:112
msgid ""
"There have been calls for LED properties such as color to be exported as "
"individual led class attributes. As a solution which doesn't incur as much "
"overhead, I suggest these become part of the device name. The naming scheme "
"above leaves scope for further attributes should they be needed. If sections "
"of the name don't apply, just leave that section blank."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:120
msgid "Keyboard backlight control LED Device Naming"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:122
msgid ""
"For backlit keyboards with a single brightness / color settings a single "
"(multicolor) LED class device should be used to allow userspace to change "
"the backlight brightness (and if possible the color). This LED class device "
"must use \"kbd_backlight\" for the function part of the LED class device "
"name. IOW the name must end with \":kbd_backlight\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:128
msgid ""
"For backlit keyboards with multiple control zones, one (multicolor) LED "
"class device should be used per zone. These LED class devices' name must "
"follow:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:131 ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:163
msgid "\"<devicename>:<color>:kbd_zoned_backlight-<zone_name>\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:133
msgid "and <devicename> must be the same for all zones of the same keyboard."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:135
msgid ""
"<zone_name> should be descriptive of which part of the keyboard backlight "
"the zone covers and should be suitable for userspace to show to an end user "
"in an UI for controlling the zones."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:139
msgid ""
"Where possible <zone_name> should be a value already used by other zoned "
"keyboards with a similar or identical zone layout, e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:142
msgid ""
"<devicename>:<color>:kbd_zoned_backlight-right <devicename>:"
"<color>:kbd_zoned_backlight-middle <devicename>:<color>:kbd_zoned_backlight-"
"left <devicename>:<color>:kbd_zoned_backlight-corners <devicename>:"
"<color>:kbd_zoned_backlight-wasd"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:148
msgid "or:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:150
msgid ""
"<devicename>:<color>:kbd_zoned_backlight-main <devicename>:"
"<color>:kbd_zoned_backlight-cursor <devicename>:<color>:kbd_zoned_backlight-"
"numpad <devicename>:<color>:kbd_zoned_backlight-corners <devicename>:"
"<color>:kbd_zoned_backlight-wasd"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:156
msgid ""
"Note that this is intended for keyboards with a limited number of zones, "
"keyboards with per key addressable backlighting must not use LED class "
"devices since the sysfs API is not suitable for rapidly change multiple LEDs "
"in one \"commit\" as is necessary to do animations / special effects on such "
"keyboards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:161
msgid "An exception to the rule that all zones must follow:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:165
msgid ""
"is made for the special case where there is a single big zone which controls "
"the backlighting of almost all of the keyboard and there are some small "
"areas with separate control, like just the 4 cursor keys, or the WASD keys. "
"In this case the main zone should use 'kbd_backlight' for the function part "
"of the name for compatibility with (older) userspace code which is not aware "
"of the \"kbd_zoned_backlight-<zone_name>\" function naming scheme."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:172
msgid ""
"While the smaller zones should use the new zoned naming scheme. Such a setup "
"would result in e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:175
msgid ""
"<devicename>:<color>:kbd_backlight <devicename>:<color>:kbd_zoned_backlight-"
"wasd"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:178
msgid ""
"\"kbd_zoned_backlight-<zone_name>\" aware userspace should be aware of this "
"exception and check for a main zone with a \"kbd_backlight\" function-name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:183
msgid "Brightness setting API"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:185
msgid "LED subsystem core exposes following API for setting brightness:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:187
msgid "led_set_brightness:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:188
msgid "it is guaranteed not to sleep, passing LED_OFF stops blinking,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:191
msgid "led_set_brightness_sync:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:192
msgid ""
"for use cases when immediate effect is desired - it can block the caller for "
"the time required for accessing device registers and can sleep, passing "
"LED_OFF stops hardware blinking, returns -EBUSY if software blink fallback "
"is enabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:199
msgid "LED registration API"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:201
msgid ""
"A driver wanting to register a LED classdev for use by other drivers / "
"userspace needs to allocate and fill a led_classdev struct and then call "
"`[devm_]led_classdev_register`. If the non devm version is used the driver "
"must call led_classdev_unregister from its remove function before free-ing "
"the led_classdev struct."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:207
msgid ""
"If the driver can detect hardware initiated brightness changes and thus "
"wants to have a brightness_hw_changed attribute then the "
"LED_BRIGHT_HW_CHANGED flag must be set in flags before registering. Calling "
"led_classdev_notify_brightness_hw_changed on a classdev not registered with "
"the LED_BRIGHT_HW_CHANGED flag is a bug and will trigger a WARN_ON."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:214
msgid "Hardware accelerated blink of LEDs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:216
msgid ""
"Some LEDs can be programmed to blink without any CPU interaction. To support "
"this feature, a LED driver can optionally implement the blink_set() function "
"(see <linux/leds.h>). To set an LED to blinking, however, it is better to "
"use the API function led_blink_set(), as it will check and implement "
"software fallback if necessary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:222
msgid ""
"To turn off blinking, use the API function led_brightness_set() with "
"brightness value LED_OFF, which should stop any software timers that may "
"have been required for blinking."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:226
msgid ""
"The blink_set() function should choose a user friendly blinking value if it "
"is called with `*delay_on==0` && `*delay_off==0` parameters. In this case "
"the driver should give back the chosen value through delay_on and delay_off "
"parameters to the leds subsystem."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:231
msgid ""
"Setting the brightness to zero with brightness_set() callback function "
"should completely turn off the LED and cancel the previously programmed "
"hardware blinking function, if any."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:236
msgid "Hardware driven LEDs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:238
msgid ""
"Some LEDs can be programmed to be driven by hardware. This is not limited to "
"blink but also to turn off or on autonomously. To support this feature, a "
"LED needs to implement various additional ops and needs to declare specific "
"support for the supported triggers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:243
msgid "With hw control we refer to the LED driven by hardware."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:245
msgid ""
"A sysfs attribute `trigger_may_offload_to_hw` is provided for userspace to "
"query supported triggers and their states."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:248
msgid "LED driver must define the following value to support hw control:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:250
msgid "hw_control_trigger:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:251
msgid "unique trigger name supported by the LED in hw control mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:254
msgid "LED driver must implement the following API to support hw control:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:255
msgid "hw_control_is_supported:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:256
msgid ""
"check if the flags passed by the supported trigger can be parsed and "
"activate hw control on the LED."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:259
msgid ""
"Return 0 if the passed flags mask is supported and can be set with "
"hw_control_set()."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:262
msgid ""
"If the passed flags mask is not supported -EOPNOTSUPP must be returned, the "
"LED trigger will use software fallback in this case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:266
msgid ""
"Return a negative error in case of any other error like device not ready or "
"timeouts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:269
msgid "hw_control_set:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:270
msgid ""
"activate hw control. LED driver will use the provided flags passed from the "
"supported trigger, parse them to a set of mode and setup the LED to be "
"driven by hardware following the requested modes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:275
msgid "Set LED_OFF via the brightness_set to deactivate hw control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:277
msgid "Return 0 on success, a negative error number on failing to apply flags."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:280
msgid "hw_control_get:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:281
msgid ""
"get active modes from a LED already in hw control, parse them and set in "
"flags the current active flags for the supported trigger."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:285
msgid ""
"Return 0 on success, a negative error number on failing parsing the initial "
"mode. Error from this function is NOT FATAL as the device may be in a not "
"supported initial state by the attached LED trigger."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:291
msgid "hw_control_get_device:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:292
msgid ""
"return the device associated with the LED driver in hw control. A trigger "
"might use this to match the returned device from this function with a "
"configured device for the trigger as the source for blinking events and "
"correctly enable hw control. (example a netdev trigger configured to blink "
"for a particular dev match the returned dev from get_device to set hw "
"control)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:301
msgid ""
"Returns a pointer to a struct device or NULL if nothing is currently "
"attached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:304
msgid "LED trigger should implement the following API to indicate hw control:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:305
msgid "hw_offloaded:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:306
msgid ""
"return a boolean indicating if the trigger is currently offloaded to "
"hardware."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:309
msgid ""
"If a trigger doesn't implement this callback, the default value will be true "
"for private triggers and false for generic ones."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:313
msgid ""
"LED driver can activate additional modes by default to workaround the "
"impossibility of supporting each different mode on the supported trigger. "
"Examples are hardcoding the blink speed to a set interval, enable special "
"feature like bypassing blink if some requirements are not met."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:318
msgid ""
"A trigger should first check if the hw control API are supported by the LED "
"driver and check if the trigger is supported to verify if hw control is "
"possible, use hw_control_is_supported to check if the flags are supported "
"and only at the end use hw_control_set to activate hw control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:323
msgid ""
"A trigger can use hw_control_get to check if a LED is already in hw control "
"and init their flags."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:326
msgid ""
"Alternatively, a private trigger can be implemented along with the LED "
"driver if the LED's hardware control doesn't fit any generic trigger. To "
"associate the private trigger with the LED classdev, their `trigger_type` "
"must be the same. To declare that the private trigger provides hardware "
"control for the associated LED classdev, set the `hw_control_trigger` string "
"to the trigger's name. Since both the LED classdev and the private trigger "
"are in the same LED driver, it's not necessary for them to coordinate via "
"`hw_control_*` callbacks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:334
msgid ""
"When the LED is in hw control, no software blink is possible and doing so "
"will effectively disable hw control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:338
msgid "Hardware-initiated trigger transition"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:340
msgid ""
"Some hardware can autonomously activate/deactivate hardware control. After "
"that, the LED hardware notifies the LED driver."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:343
msgid ""
"If the driver can detect such transitions and thus wants to notify the LED "
"core to update the current trigger then the `LED_TRIG_HW_CHANGED` flag must "
"be set in flags before registering. To update the current trigger "
"accordingly, call `led_trigger_notify_hw_control_changed` on the LED "
"classdev."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:348
msgid ""
"This capability is restricted to the LED device's private trigger. The "
"private trigger must have been properly registered (see above) and named "
"after `hw_control_trigger`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:352
msgid "Only two transitions are defined:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:354
msgid "\"none\" => private trigger:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:355
msgid ""
"This happens when the hardware autonomously activates hardware control and "
"when \"none\" (i.e., no trigger) is currently active. If the private trigger "
"is already active when the method is called, this is essentially a no-op."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:360
msgid ""
"The activation sequence for the private trigger will be executed as normal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:363
msgid ""
"The LED driver and its private trigger must be able to handle the activation "
"sequence even if the hardware is currently in hardware control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:367
msgid ""
"If error occurs in the activation sequence, the LED Trigger core reverts the "
"effective trigger to \"none\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:370
msgid "private trigger => \"none\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:371
msgid ""
"This happens when the hardware autonomously deactivates hardware control and "
"when the private trigger is currently active. If \"none\" (i.e., no trigger) "
"is active when the method is called, this is essentially a no-op."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:376
msgid ""
"The deactivation sequence for the private trigger will be executed as "
"normal, except that the current LED brightness is retained. The reason for "
"keeping the brightness unchanged is that some hardware may choose a specific "
"brightness instead of simply turning off the LED after autonomously "
"deactivating hardware control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:382
msgid ""
"The LED driver and its private trigger must be able to handle the "
"deactivation sequence even if the hardware is not currently in hardware "
"control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:386
msgid ""
"If the current trigger is neither the private trigger nor \"none\", no "
"transition will be made."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:390
msgid "Known Issues"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../leds/leds-class.rst:392
msgid ""
"The LED Trigger core cannot be a module as the simple trigger functions "
"would cause nightmare dependency issues. I see this as a minor issue "
"compared to the benefits the simple trigger functionality brings. The rest "
"of the LED subsystem can be modular."
msgstr ""
